Feature Extraction of Text Data using TF-IDF Vectorizer
What is TF-IDF?

TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency) converts text into numerical features by measuring the importance of words within a collection of documents.

#example 

from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer

documents = [
    "Machine learning is awesome",
    "Machine learning uses data",
    "Data science and machine learning"
]

vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer()

X = vectorizer.fit_transform(documents)

print(vectorizer.get_feature_names_out())
print(X.toarray())

## Numerical Dataset Pre-Processing
What is Numerical Data Pre-Processing?

Preparing numerical features by:

Handling missing values
Feature scaling
Data transformation


# example 
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

data = pd.DataFrame({
    'Age': [25, 30, None, 40, 35]
})

# Handle Missing Values
imputer = SimpleImputer(strategy='mean')
data['Age'] = imputer.fit_transform(data[['Age']])

# Standardization
scaler = StandardScaler()
data['Age'] = scaler.fit_transform(data[['Age']])

print(data)

# Text Dataset Pre-Processing
What is Text Pre-Processing?

Text data often contains noise and inconsistencies. Pre-processing helps clean and standardize text before feature extraction.

#example 
import re

text = "Machine Learning is AMAZING!!!"

# Convert to lowercase
text = text.lower()

# Remove special characters
text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\s]', '', text)

print(text)
